course: dictionary
lesson: 8743
title: One word, many jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it, which is the context. «A website is stored on a computer called a server, and your device asks the server for it.» Here a server is a computer, because a computer can store a website.
explain: «In tennis, the server hits the ball to start each point.» The sentence is about a game, so the server is a player. Context always tells you which meaning is right.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. If you think a server is a waiter, does «the server brought a website» make sense? It does not, so the meaning must be the computer.
fact: A server is a powerful computer that stores websites, files, games or emails and sends them to other computers.
fact: «A server is a computer that provides a service, such as web pages, videos or a game world; your own device is called the client.»
fact: In a restaurant, a server is a waiter or waitress who brings food and drink. In tennis, the server is the player who starts a point.
fact: «Salad servers» are spoons or tools for putting food on plates.
exercise: «A website is stored on a computer called a server, and your device asks the server for it.» What is a server in this sentence? | A tool for putting food on plates | A player who hits the ball | A waiter who brings food | *A computer that stores websites and sends them
exercise: «In tennis, the server hits the ball to start each point.» Who is the server? | A ball boy | A referee who counts the score | A tennis net | *The player who starts the point
exercise: A server provides a service, such as web pages or a game world. What is your own device called? | The server | *The client | The data centre | The network
test: «Salad servers» are used for what? | *To put food on plates | To store games | To hit a ball | To ask for a web page
test: What is a data centre? | A tennis court | A restaurant kitchen | *A building full of computers called servers | A salad bowl
exam: When Ruby types a web address, what does DNS turn the name into? | A game server | A tennis point | A waiter's order | *An IP address number
